     1	# Chapter 9 — Within the System
     2	
     3	The residence's small room was not a room anyone had chosen for councils. It had been the winter store when the residence was a farmhouse, and it kept the shape: one door, one shuttered window, a stone floor with a drain in the corner that had drained something else three hundred years ago, and a table too big for it that could not be got out again because the door had been narrowed at some point by somebody who did not think about tables. The delegation used it for the same reason Seln had approved of it the first time he was brought there, which was that its one window faced the wall of the stores range four feet away and its walls were three feet of stone, and a voice in it went nowhere at all.
     4	
     5	They had used it four times that term. The first three had been legal. This one was not.
     6	
     7	Lira had asked for it on the second morning after the ruling and had asked for it in the delegation's own grammar — a note on the residence board, *small room, Fifth-day, after supper, five* — and everyone who read the board understood that a note requesting five people by number rather than by name was a note about the fifth. Karis had brought the proceedings' papers because she brought them everywhere, and had put them face-down on the far table before anyone sat, which was her own arrangement and which she made without comment. Brom had brought the tea and the room's one good chair, carried in on his shoulder from the common room because the small room's chairs were benches and Brom had opinions about how long a council could run on a bench. Seln had come in last and taken the bench's middle, which was where he had sat at every table for a year and a half, and had put nothing on the table in front of him at all.
     8	
     9	Cael counted the room, because counting rooms was what he did on the way into things.
    10	
    11	Five people. One lamp, turned to three-quarters. Two years, four months since the four of them had first sat at a table together in a boarding-house in Ardenmere with a legal theory and no money. Twelve days since the seal had said *lawful* and *proceeding* in the same paragraph.
    12	
    13	Lira put it on the table on the first night of the term's second half, and she put it there plainly, because plainness was the only register she trusted for a thing this size.
    14	
    15	"The proceedings are a corridor. Karis drew it; the ruling confirmed it; every door opens one way and the seal has said so in its own type." She had the drill-book on her knee and did not look at it. "The day-after is being inventoried — Seln's five offices, Hesk's shop, the ninth category. The Registry Line is four days east of Ostrand. I've checked the road." She looked around the room — the four of them and Seln at the bench's middle, one lamp, the proceedings' papers on the far table face-down by her own arrangement. "So I'm asking the question out loud, once, so it's been asked: why are we still filing responses?"
    16	
    17	"Checked it how," said Karis.
    18	
    19	"Properly." Lira turned her hand over on her knee, which was the gesture she used when she had done more work than she intended to be praised for. "Three carters at the ferry landing who run the east road for a living, one at a time, on three different days, about three different things, because a woman asking about the same road three times in one week is a woman a carter remembers. It's four days to the Line from Ostrand with a wagon and three without. Six waystations, five of them registry-provisioned, the sixth private and past the last station. The Line isn't a line — there's no fence, no post, nothing. It's where the last Arbiter station is, and then there isn't another one, and the carters know exactly where it stops because that's where their manifests stop being checked." She let that sit for a second. "Two of them go past it. They said the same thing in different words: nobody out there asks what tier you are, because there's nobody to ask and nothing to check it against."
    20	
    21	"And the third?"
    22	
    23	"The third won't go past it and wouldn't say why." Lira's mouth did something that was not a smile. "So that's on the ledger too."
    24	
    25	It was the earlier question matured — *what we do the day after* — and the room had known it was coming since the coach ride from the third session, and the room took it the way the found family took its hardest things, which was honestly, in four voices, plus one silence.
    26	
    27	---
    28	
    29	Karis argued the record, and she argued it standing, with the formality she reserved for findings she did not like, because she did not like this one either.
    30	
    31	"Every institution we have ever been inside failed us until text held it accountable. Denvash put him out a back door with no record. Ardenmere's circuit rated him because Vell kept a ledger. Greyvane's hearing found for him because the clause was in a compilation nobody had read. Halcenvane enrolled him because the record was true and Bracken wouldn't falsify it. Norhold fielded him on a sentence." She counted them on her fingers, the way she counted precedents. "Text is the only thing that has ever held. Not once — *every time.*" She set her hand flat on the face-down papers. "And the record of walking out mid-proceedings reads *fled.* It's the registry's word. I've read it in two hundred files. *Respondent fled the jurisdiction pending ruling* — and after it, in every file, the ruling goes in anyway, by default, and the default is the petition's whole prayer, granted, with a line beneath it that says the respondent's flight is *itself evidence of the integrity question.*" She sat down. "Leave, and we hand them the confession they couldn't get from Umber, from Vastin, from Daeva, from eleven pounds of the truth. That's the record. I'm the archive. I'm telling you what the archive says."
    32	
    33	"And if the archive's wrong this time?" Lira said.
    34	
    35	"Then it's wrong on the text, and I'll have been wrong with a perfect filing on the table, and I've said that I'd rather that than the alternative." Karis's hands were flat and her voice was not quite. "I know what the corridor is. I drew it. I'm asking you to consider that walking out of a corridor is also a thing the corridor was built to make you do."
    36	
    37	"Say more about that," said Cael.
    38	
    39	"I read a file at Ternhall in my second year that I've thought about every week since." She did not open the notebook; she did not need it. "A guild practitioner in a coastal district, integrity matter, nine years ago. Ninety pages of proceedings, and the respondent was winning — genuinely winning; I checked the rulings, they're sound — and on the fourteenth session he stopped attending. Not fled. He wrote a letter first, a good one, saying he had lost confidence in the process and would take no further part. The registry entered the letter, thanked him for it, and ruled by default the same week." She squared her hands on the table's edge. "The default ruling is two paragraphs long, and the second one cites the letter. His own reasoning for leaving, quoted, as evidence of the thing he was accused of. They didn't twist it. They didn't need to. A man who says he doesn't trust the instrument has said, in the instrument's own grammar, that the instrument's confidence in him was correctly withheld."
    40	
    41	The room was quiet.
    42	
    43	"So what's the answer to that?" Brom said.
    44	
    45	"There isn't one," said Karis. "That's why I've thought about it every week. The only thing the file proves is that leaving is a move the machine has a form for. It doesn't prove the move is wrong. It proves it isn't free, and I'm tired of being the only person at this table who prices things nobody wants priced."
    46	
    47	"You're not the only one," said Lira.
    48	
    49	"No," Karis agreed. "You're just the one who's willing to pay it, and I'm the one who's going to make sure you know the number first."
    50	
    51	---
    52	
    53	Brom argued the middle, from the room's one good chair, in the register of weight, and he argued it in three sentences because three were what he had.
    54	
    55	"Prepare both roads." He turned his cup. "Karis files. The counsel argues. We win the sessions, because we do. And every night, after the filing, somebody packs one box." He looked at Lira. "You've checked the road east. Check it properly. Waystations, the Line, what's past it. Not to walk it. To know it." He looked at Cael. "You've never once gone into a fight without knowing the exits. This is a fight. Same law." And to the room: "Walk neither yet. Both roads open. That's all I've got."
    56	
    57	"That's a lot," said Lira.
    58	
    59	"It's three sentences," said Brom, and the room let him have it, because it was, and because it was also the first time any of them had said the word *box* aloud, and the word stayed in the room after he said it.
    60	
    61	Lira picked it up, after a moment, the way she picked up anything left on a floor.
    62	
    63	"What's in yours?"
    64	
    65	Brom considered it seriously, which was the only way he considered anything.
    66	
    67	"Clothes. The Copper certificate, because it's the only paper my father will accept as an answer to a question he asked when I was fifteen. My mother's letter." He turned the cup back the other way. "And the strapping. Two years of it, all of it, because you can buy strapping anywhere and I don't want to be somewhere I have to."
    68	
    69	"That's not much for a Velmere," said Lira, carefully.
    70	
    71	"No," Brom agreed. "It's the second box that's the problem."
    72	
    73	Nobody asked what the second box was. Cael charted the not-asking, and charted the way Brom let the not-asking stand, and wrote later that the second box was the review — the advancement review, docketed for term's end, the thing his family's ledger had demanded since he was a child and which he had never once mentioned in the same conversation as the word *east* — and that Brom had put it on the table by refusing to, which was the whole of the man's grammar.
    74	
    75	---
    76	
    77	Cael argued the first pole, and he argued it at full strength, because it was the strongest position he had ever held and he had three years of receipts for it and the receipts were real.
    78	
    79	"The hearing at Greyvane. The demonstration provision. Four semester stamps, renewed, on a Compact-observed record. An inspection that declined to find. A tournament their own clause put me in, and a match their own instrument couldn't score, and the instrument's keeper writing *no number goes here* rather than a lie." He said them in order, the way he wrote inventories, because the order was the argument. "Every time the people failed, the text held. Not because text is kind — because text is *there.* You can read it. You can find the orphaned clause. You can put a true record on a counter and make a registrar decline to falsify it. The people are weather. The text is ground."
    80	
    81	"Say the rest of it," said Karis. "You've got a fifth one you're not saying."
    82	
    83	"Denvash," Cael said. "A man in an office decided what I was in eleven seconds and there was no text in the room, and I was fourteen, and it took me three years and four institutions to get a sentence written down that undid it. Every good thing in my life since has been text somebody wrote down and then had to honor." He looked at Lira, whose question it was. "The system has never beaten me when the text was in the room. I'm not leaving the room while the text still works."
    84	
