     2	
     3	The annex did not happen the next morning, because the destabilization did, and the postponement was conducted the way everything between Cael and Seln was conducted: in a floor schedule.
     4	
     5	The note came up from the assessment office at the seventh bell, on the counter's ordinary stock, in the counter's ordinary hand, addressed to the delegation's floor coordinator and copied nowhere: *Wing three practice allocation revised. Enrollee's supervised hours suspended four days pending recovery-of-form. Reallocation to follow.* It was a true document about a true thing and it said nothing whatever, and Cael read it lying flat on his back on the residence floor with his boots still on, because the floor was the only surface in the room that had stopped moving, and thought, with the part of him that was still able to draft sentences, that the man had built a postponement out of a scheduling instrument in under an hour and had probably not thought of it as difficult.
     6	
     7	The cascade had come on before dawn, real, on the constraint's own clock — the eighth acquisition's bill presenting mid-recovery the way the log had learned to expect and the body never quite did — and Cael managed it with the discipline three years had built: recovery days banked in advance, the circle warned the night before, nothing evaluable scheduled, the room cleared of everything that could be dropped, water and bread within reach of the bed, the door left unlatched so that nobody would have to break it.
     8	
     9	It came anyway, and it came strange, because the Anchor-adjacent component pulled like nothing before it.
    10	
    11	The prior architectures had wanted things he understood. Wind wanted motion — it sat under a walk and made the walk want to be a run. Ember wanted contact, and had spent three days after its arrival making every surface in a room feel like a thing that could be asked. Storm had wanted, heavily, for nine weeks, the medium itself: the air in a corridor presenting as country, folds proposing themselves along every draft. All of those were appetites he could name, and a named appetite could be scheduled around.
    12	
    13	The Anchor-adjacent wanted *fixity*.
    14	
    15	It wanted to fix points, and it did not much care in what. For two days the doorways of the residence went subtly wrong at the edges of his attention — thresholds presenting themselves to some new sense as *anchorable*, lines of the room's geometry offering to be bound: the window's frame, the stair's turn at the second landing, the gap between the table and the wall, the four corners of a washstand, the exact place where the door's shadow crossed the floorboard's seam. Every aperture in his ordinary world raising its hand. It was not painful and it was not loud. It was worse than either, because it was *reasonable* — the component did not shout at him, it made small courteous proposals, at the rate of perhaps forty an hour, each one of them a place that could be held, and the whole difficulty of it was that a thing which proposes forty times an hour and is never wrong about the geometry is a thing you begin to agree with.
    16	
    17	He agreed with it once, on the second morning, and that was the incident that went into the record.
    18	
