===== books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-16.md =====
     1	# Chapter 16 — Two Hundred Years
     2	
     3	Karis had the cache for the second night, and she mapped it the way she had spent her whole working life preparing to map the primary source of it — against everything she carried, in her formal register, findings entered one at a time — and each finding, as it landed, landed on a thread somebody at that table had been carrying for years.
     4	
     5	She had done the work in the intervening day, and the intervening day was the part nobody watched. Nine hours in the locked annex with the door wedged and a jug of water and no lunch. Three hundred and eighty documents laid out in dated courses on a floor because the table was not long enough, walked between in stockinged feet with a slate in one hand. Her own four-year working archive brought down in three crates and opened beside them: the Greyvane hearing's compiled record, the copied Ardenmere passages, the registry-law commonplace books, the standardization directive's own founding text in the form she had transcribed it at Prynn, and the founding-era compilation with the Greyvane signatures that had arrived from Prynn's archive the week before on a request the delegation's budget had funded and Withrow had signed without asking why.
     6	
     7	She had built an index. That was the whole of the intervening day, and she described it to them at the start of the second night in one sentence, because she wanted them to understand what she had and had not done: "I have not interpreted anything yet. I have made it possible to interpret. Everything I am about to say is a comparison between two documents that are both on this table, and if I say a thing you cannot see, stop me."
     8	
     9	She worked from the oldest stratum up, because provenance ran downward and she trusted the floor of a case more than its lid. The lamp. The memoranda in dated courses. The maintenance leaf in its wax-paper sleeve, apart, handled with the specific care she gave documents that could not be replaced. Cael at the table's end with the observation notebook and the anomaly's question, because the night would have a check in it only he could run. Seln at the wall, because it was his case and a keeper attends his archive. Lira and Brom present and quiet, the way the family sat with things too large to help with.
    10	
    11	---
    12	
    13	**Finding one — the mechanism and the flag.**
    14	
    15	The maintenance leaf's *advisory corrections function* lived, its own text said, in the evaluation sub-layer — the stratum of the Arbiter protocol below the reading, below the classification, the maintenance level a practitioner never perceived and an evaluator never touched. The corrections fired at Kindling: after the architecture was read and before the classification reached a record a human saw, the function rewrote inconvenient architecture into a lesser Path, so that the falsification never occurred in a document, never crossed a desk, never required a corrupt official — because by the time any official saw the classification, the classification was already the lie, clean, native, indistinguishable from a true reading to every instrument that ran on the same sub-layer.
    16	
    17	She built it for them in four steps, on the slate, in chalk, because Brom had asked her to say it in a way he could hold.
    18	
    19	One: a child stands in a station and the protocol reads the architecture. Two: the reading is true, and exists, for an interval nobody has ever measured, inside a layer nobody reads. Three: the function consults something — a schedule, a rule, a list; the leaf does not say which, and she wrote *unknown* on the slate in the same chalk and underlined it — and where the reading is inconvenient, writes a lesser one in its place. Four: the classification emerges. An assessor reads it aloud. A clerk enters it. A certificate issues. A family goes home.
    20	
    21	"That's why no living official knows," Karis said. "There's nothing to know. No one falsifies anything. The falsification happened before the record existed, in a layer no one reads, on a schedule no one runs. Every official in the chain handles a true document. The document is just true about a lie." She set the leaf down. "It's the most elegant corruption I have ever seen described, and I want it on the record that elegant is the correct word and that I hate using it."
    22	
    23	"The station clerk in the river town," Brom said.
    24	
    25	"The station clerk in the river town was telling the truth. So was the form. So was the certificate." She did not soften it. "That's the design. There is nobody to be angry at anywhere in that file, and a girl in a river town is thirty-three years old and living somebody else's life."
    26	
    27	Lira asked the question that had been sitting under the table since the night before, and she asked it the way she asked everything, which was without a cushion. "Inconvenient to who?"
    28	
    29	"That's the second finding I'd have made if the leaf said more, and it doesn't." Karis pulled two courses of the reclassification stack toward her. "But the orders do. Forty pairs, and I sorted them last night by what the *true* reading described rather than by date, which nobody has ever had the material to do." She turned the sorted courses so the table could see the shape. "Twenty-six of the forty are corrections downward from architectures with one property in common: reach. Perception at distance, effect at distance, influence over a medium somebody else is standing in. Not power. Reach. The rest are miscellaneous, and eight of those I'd argue about."
    30	
    31	"So it isn't about strength," Cael said.
    32	
    33	"It has never once been about strength. There's a filed Iron in this stack whose true reading would have put her three grades up, and the machinery let a very strong Bronze in the same district go through untouched, because the strong one's architecture couldn't do anything at a distance except be strong." She squared the courses. "Whatever wrote that function wasn't worried about people who could hit hard. It was worried about people who could see, or reach, or hold something they weren't standing next to. Which is a preference. Which means somebody had a *policy*, two hundred years ago, about what kind of practitioner the continent was allowed to have — and every Kindling since has been quietly enforcing it, one fourteen-year-old at a time, in a layer nobody reads."
    34	
    35	"How many fourteen-year-olds?" said Brom.
    36	
    37	"Take the forty as a sample of nothing, because it is a sample of nothing — it's what one man happened to touch across nineteen postings." Karis put her pen down, which she did when she wanted a sentence heard as an estimate and not a figure. "But there are roughly nine hundred district stations. Two hundred years. If it fired on one child in five hundred, it would still be more people than have died in every war on this continent since the founding, and they'd all be alive, and none of them would know."
    38	
    39	Seln, at the wall: "Which is why I stopped estimating."
    40	
    41	And then the case the function could not handle: an architecture with no lesser bin.
    42	
    43	She laid the proceedings transcript beside the leaf — the returns Ilsev's referrals had produced, entered in the record over years, their exact text: *no originating authority of record; designation predates file creation* — *referral closed at registry level; no further inquiry authorized* — and she laid beside those the Suppression-Advisory Watch Level 4 flag's own history, all of it, the whole accumulated dossier of a thing that had followed a boy since he was fourteen: Havel's carried note, Ilsev's paper trail, the designation nobody could trace, the two referrals, the two returns, the flag no analyst had been able to read, the citation the Norhold convening had used.
    44	
    45	"Look at what the returns actually say," she said, "and stop hearing them as bureaucracy for one minute. *No originating authority of record.* That is not a stonewall. A stonewall says *the requesting officer lacks standing* or *the matter is above your grade*. This says there is no author. *Designation predates file creation* — the flag is older than the file it sits in, which is administratively impossible unless the flag came from somewhere other than the file's own chain. And *referral closed at registry level; no further inquiry authorized* — closed by nobody, at a level, with an authorization that names no one. Three returns, four years, two offices, and not one human name anywhere in any of them."
    46	
    47	She let that stand for a moment and then delivered the sentence the whole night had been walking toward, the transcript in one hand and the leaf in the other:
    48	
    49	"When the Arbiter meets an architecture it cannot reclassify into a lesser Path — because [SHATTERED] has no lesser bin; there is nothing beneath it to correct it into — the corrections function *cannot fire*. It has no output for the input. And what fires instead is the contingency: the suppression-advisory flag. The machinery's fallback for the one case it cannot falsify. The flag isn't surveillance. It's the corrections function failing safe — flagging the anomaly it couldn't correct, so the layer's protection behavior can handle by exception what the layer couldn't handle by rule." She set both documents down. "And the protection behavior is what Ilsev has been photographing for three years. The returns — *no authority of record*, *closed, no further inquiry* — are the sub-layer defending itself. Auto-closing inquiries no living official authorized, because no living official runs that layer. She's been interrogating the mechanism for three years and filing its answers." A breath. "She just never knew what was answering."
    50	
    51	Nobody spoke.
    52	
    53	Seln, at the wall, said one thing, level and unhurried, and it was the corroboration the finding needed and the only one available anywhere. "I have submitted four inquiries in my career that returned in that grammar. Three were about classification anomalies. One was about a Level 4 designation on a subject in a coastal posting nineteen years ago." A pause. "I did not connect them. I filed them as institutional obstruction, which is what obstruction looks like from inside, and I was wrong for nineteen years about what had answered me."
    54	
    55	Cael entered the finding whole, and beside it the thing the finding did to four years of his life: *the flag on my file since the day I Kindled — the thing Havel found and was told to stand down about, the thing Coss couldn't read, the thing that came back "no authority" and then "no inquiry" — was never a person deciding to watch me. It was the machine failing to correct me and flagging that it couldn't. I've been carrying a surveillance order my whole life that no one ever signed because no one ever gave it. The layer gave it. To itself.*
    56	
    57	He sat with the pen down for a while after writing it. What he was doing, he decided afterward, was the small and stupid work of dismantling a face — because there had been a face on it, for four years, without his ever having built one on purpose: someone, somewhere, in an office, who had decided he was worth watching. He had hated that person in a low steady way since he was fifteen. There was no person. There had never been a person. And the thing that was actually there was worse in a way he could not immediately price, because a person can be argued with, and a person retires.
    58	
    59	Neither Ilsev nor Havel would ever be told. That was settled in eleven words at the table — Karis proposed it, nobody dissented, Seln said "correct" — and the reasons were three and all of them were about the carriers: telling Ilsev destroyed a woman's entire understanding of the instrument she served in exchange for nothing she could act on; telling Havel put a note six entries deep into the hands of a man who would then have to decide whether to file it; and both disclosures widened a circle that could not afford one more chair. The two of them would go on being what they had been all along, which was honest, and unknowing, and load-bearing.
