   118	
   119	---
   120	
   121	Withrow was informed alone, in her office, by Cael, and she received it standing.
   122	
   123	The office was as it had been for two years — the file cases, the framed charter above them, the desk with nothing on it that was not in use — except that the term's correspondence had colonized the long side table in stacks that had a system Cael could read at a glance and would not have been able to read two years ago: petitions, responses, guild inquiries, and one stack, the smallest, of letters from other chancellors, which he understood without being told were the ones that had cost her the most to answer.
   124	
   125	She heard the whole shape out without interrupting, standing at the desk's end with her hands at her sides, the respondent chancellor learning her academy's defense would end by resignation.
   126	
   127	Her registry-trained arithmetic ran it in front of him, because she owed him the arithmetic and it was the only comfort her register could offer, which was to be exact. "The resignation protects Halcenvane. The proceedings become moot — there is no enrollment to re-evaluate, no respondent institution to find against, no charter file entry, because you cannot revoke an enrollment that has been surrendered. The asset-restriction cannot execute against a person outside the registry's jurisdiction. You forfeit nothing that was not already forfeit under the ruling, and you take the academy off the same seal as your name, which is the thing I could not ask you to do and the thing you are doing anyway." She set the ruling's copy down. "As registry arithmetic, it is the correct answer. I have checked it four times, the way I check everything about you, and it is correct."
   128	
   129	"You checked it four times before I came up."
   130	
   131	"I checked it four times the night the ruling landed," she said. "It is my office to know the respondent's best remaining move before the respondent does. It is not my office to recommend it, because a chancellor who recommends resignation to an enrollee under proceedings has done the petitioner's work for him at no charge, and I would have had to enter that in my own ledger, and I keep that ledger the way Bracken keeps his." She squared the copy to the desk's edge. "So I have known the answer for four days and said nothing, and waited to see whether you would arrive at it, and you have arrived at it, and I am now permitted to tell you that it is correct. That is the whole of what the office allows me, and I want you to understand that I have used every inch of it."
   132	
   133	Then the other column, unperformed, because she did not perform things.
   134	
