   124	
   125	He caught himself, somewhere around the tenth bell, doing what he did — running the room as an instrument, itemizing it, filing it — and made the decision he had learned at a scarred table by a harbor window to let himself make perhaps three times a year, and put the instrument down. No count. No log. He sat in a common room he was going to lose, with five people who knew both his ledgers and one who knew neither and had come anyway, and did nothing at all with any of it for the better part of an hour.
   126	
   127	The evening ran until the twelfth bell. Ephram sang the lock-keeper song and made Brom sing the second verse, which Brom did, in a bass that surprised nobody who had ever heard him breathe. Lira put her strapped foot on a chair and refused to explain it. Karis argued with Ephram for twenty minutes about whether a cohort tally constituted a record within the meaning of anything, and lost on the merits and said so. Seln left first, at the tenth bell, with a nod that took in the whole table, and at the door he stopped and turned back and said, to the room and not to anyone, "It is a good room. I have been in a great many." Then he went, and the door closed, and Lira looked at the door and then at Cael and did not say the thing she was thinking, because the paper on the far table was face-down and would stay face-down until morning.
   128	
   129	---
   130	
   131	The inventory went into the satchel's first use that night, at the window, the new leather's smell in the room and the fountain running below, and it was the fullest the log had ever carried:
   132	
