   134	
   135	*One anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Turned over once, per the custom at intervals that matter. Still open. Still real. Patience.*
   136	
   137	*One term, five sittings spent, one ruling pending, one road cut behind us, one case across the campus, down from its shelf.*
   138	
   139	*Eighteen. Eight fragments. Tomorrow, Seln's annex. Hesk says mind the shape. I think tomorrow is the shape.*
   140	
   141	He capped the pen and put the log into the pocket built for it and closed the flap, which closed against nothing, because it was not raining. Then he set the satchel on the sill and left it there, where the cold came through the glass and would keep the leather honest.
   142	
   143	It sat in the window's square with the fountain going under it: waxed, strapped, brass at every buckle, the spare one flat on the strap's underside where it would not chafe — the tool built for the road he was actually on, a shape that did not belong in a residence room and had never once pretended to. And what came back to him, looking at it, was not Hesk's note or Brom's ten years or the term's arithmetic. It was a man getting up from a table at the tenth bell and stopping at the door on his way out.
