   287	"Come tomorrow," she said. "Rooke'll let you, if I ask him. He let you stand in the door all week." She was not looking at him; she was looking across the empty court at the fountain. "Not the gallery. And not the rail either. The door, behind me, where I can't see you from my chalk. I want you there, and I don't want to look up and find you counting."
   288	
   289	"I'll stand in the door."
   290	
   291	"And don't write it down while it's happening. After, if you like. Not during."
   292	
   293	"Not during."
   294	
   295	She nodded. Then she took a breath, and let it out, and said, in a much lighter voice, that the gardener's dog had more sense than the gardener, and that she had half a mind to go down after supper and tell them both so. She talked about the dog the whole way to the refectory, and he let her.

