     1	# Chapter 43 — The Glide
     2	
     3	He stood in the door, as he had promised, with his hands in his coat and nothing in them.
     4	
     5	The east hall had its two lamps lit and its shutters down. Outside it was not yet light, and a wet wind was leaning on the north wall, so that the long room seemed smaller than it was, a lit box with six people in it and the dark pressing on the glass. The program's clerk had moved her trestle out from the wall to the edge of the painted floor and sat at it with the red book open and two pens laid ready. Rooke sat on her right with his board. On her left the Stone stood with a flag in each hand, a red and a blue, held down along his thighs like a man carrying two fish.
     6	
     7	The Force leaned on the far rail with his arms folded; he had nothing to do this morning but watch, and he had dressed for it, in a clean shirt.
     8	
     9	Lira was at her chalk at the south end with her back to the door. She had taped her own wrists but not the ankle, because the healer had said there was nothing left in it to tape, and she stood now with her weight even on both feet and her left arm tucked a little toward her ribs, from the old habit, and did not look round.
    10	
    11	The Current girl came to the north chalk on her toes.
    12	
    13	She was a third-year, Iron Rank Four, long in the leg and narrow everywhere, and Cael had stood in front of her himself once, in wing three, under four strangers' pencils. A Current's Path took the catch out of the floor under her. She did not run across boards; she went over them, level, the way water goes over a weir, and when she turned she did not turn so much as bend, leaning into a long smooth curve with her arms out for balance and her heels never once coming down. She had drilled on the cross beside Lira's since the first morning of the program. She knew the four-count as well as Lira knew it. She shook her hands loose at her sides, and looked at Lira down the length of the floor, and grinned, a little nervously, and then stopped grinning because Rooke had looked at her.
    14	
    15	"Program trial," said Rooke. "My rule. You both know it, and I'll say it anyway, because it goes in the book." He did not get up. "Four exchanges. A flag to whoever takes each one, or to nobody. Most flags has it. Level after four, the table rates you both. Three bursts for the Wind; past three the card pays. The Current, what her Path allows." He nodded at the clerk. "Rated. In this book. Go when the Stone drops his hands."
    16	
    17	The Stone raised both flags to his shoulders, held them there for the space of a breath, and dropped them.
    18	
    19	Lira ran the count.
    20	
    21	It was the thing she did best in the world, and for three beats of it Cael forgot that she had ever been away. She lied with the front foot, a weight laid on it and left a moment too long. She lied with the back. She lied with her shoulders, a half-turn toward the east wall that pulled the Current girl's whole long glide a yard that way, and the Current girl, who knew perfectly well that it was a lie and had been lied to by it two hundred times, went all the same, because her Path did not stop for knowing. She leaned into her bend.
    22	
    23	On the fourth, Lira went.
    24	
    25	Burst one, low and flat off the left foot, not across the glide but along the inside of its curve, the line she had found on this floor before the first sitting and beaten this girl's pattern with on more mornings than either of them could count. It was right, and Cael saw that it was right from the door. At the far end of that line there was a place on the boards where the Current girl's bend would bring her hip, and Lira would be standing in that place a quarter of a beat before the hip arrived, with her palm out.
    26	
