     1	# Chapter 45 — The Lake House
     2	
     3	Bracken brought the schedule to the old frames himself, on the Sixth-day, in the hour after the noon meal when he knew Brom would be there.
     4	
     5	He came in by the yard door with a single sheet in his hand and his spectacles pushed up on his forehead, and stood a moment inside it while his eyes found the three of them: Ephram on the long frame, in the middle of a set; Brom beside the short one with his arms folded; Cael against the wall. The old frames stood in a low stone room behind the stables that had been a harness store once and still smelled of it, with two lamps, a sanded floor, and the two great timber frames with their iron bars and springs, older than anybody on the hill.
     6	
     7	"From the district seat," said Bracken. "By this morning's bag. It is addressed to the practitioner, care of the registrar, and so I have read it, and so I have walked it across a wet yard, which I do not do for everything." He held it out. "It is the review's schedule of evidence."
     8	
     9	Brom unfolded his arms and took it, and read it standing, without moving his lips, and then he held it a little away from him and read it aloud, slowly, the way he read anything official, as if testing each plank before he put his weight on it.
    10	
    11	"*Ostrand district registry. Review for advancement of tier: Brom, enrolled at Halcenvane. Schedule of evidence. The panel will read, first, the practitioner's record as entered, the continental title included. Second, the house's attendance and floor sheets for the two years preceding, to be supplied by the registrar under the house's seal. Third, a reading in the sigil frame, under load, taken on the day in the presence of the three assessors. No testimony is contemplated. Sitting: the last Fifth-day of the academic year, at the second bell.*"
    12	
    13	He lowered it.
    14	
    15	"The second is mine," said Bracken. "I have supplied it. It went down this morning by the return bag and weighed four pounds nine ounces, and there is not a blot in it." He pulled his spectacles down onto his nose. "The first is the registry's own. The third is yours, and nobody can supply it for you." He looked at the short frame, and then at Brom's left shoulder, briefly, as he might have glanced at a column he had already added. "I take it that is what this room is for."
    16	
    17	"It's what it's been for all year," said Brom.
    18	
    19	"Then I shall leave you to it." Bracken turned to go, and turned back, as he always did. "Under load," he said. "I asked the district clerk what load. He wrote back that it is the assessors' to set on the day, and that in his experience they set it at what the title was won against, and a little over." He nodded once to each of them, the small nod, and went out into the yard.
    20	
    21	Nobody said anything until the door had shut.
    22	
    23	Then Ephram, who had not stopped his set, finished it, and let the long frame's bar come back to its rest with both hands, and went to the rack by the wall where the short frame's iron plates hung on their pegs. He took down two, and looked at them, and hung one back, and took down a heavier one in its place.
    24	
    25	He did not ask what the title had been won against. A man who had kept two years of somebody else's figures on three sheets of ruled paper was not likely to have missed Brom's.
    26	
