    32	
    33	Cael had watched Brom hold things for years. He had never before watched him practise it.
    34	
    35	The short frame was a simple machine: two timber uprights, a crossbar of iron that ran in greased slots between them, and under each end of the bar a spring as thick as a man's wrist. You stood beneath the bar and took it on your shoulders, or on your forearms crossed above your head, and somebody let the catches go, and the springs drove it down. A sigil frame at a district seat was a finer thing, with a plate that read what the practitioner's Path did under the weight. This one read nothing. It only pressed.
    36	
