books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md:25:Rooke's clerk at the north table with the rating sheet. Rooke at the floor's head. Gault at the south tier's foot, in the seat the evaluator of record took at rated bouts, with nothing in his hands, which was not the way Gault attended anything. Withrow at the north tier's second row, not in the chancellor's place, which was a decision. Brom, Karis, Lira at the west rail; Seln at the east tier's top, where the sightlines to every entrance converged.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md:106:Gault's was a movement of the head at the south tier's foot, once, at the flag, and then the evaluator of record went away up the covered walk with nothing in his hands, having attended a rated bout without a form, which four separate members of the coaching staff remarked on and none of them understood.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-04.md:111:The proceedings' first session convened at Ostrand on the fourth Fifth-day of the month, and it was procedural, cold, and brief, and it was the coldest hour of Cael's institutional life, because nothing in it was aimed at him.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-03.md:53:Cael put the whole instrument on him for the rest of the conference, and ran it the way he had run it on every official who had ever held a decision about him: the hands first, then the breath, then the eyes at the moment of the sentence that ought to cost something, then the half-beat before an answer that a man spends deciding how much to give. Four measures, four years of practice, and the readings came back in order — hands still, breath level, eyes on the document not the person, no half-beat at all — and the fourth null was the one that mattered. There was no deciding. The answers arrived at the speed of the questions because nothing in the man was choosing between versions of them.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-15.md:71:**How he walked away from the job.** In the only way his trade allowed, which was by staying in it and keeping. A man in his position could not resign; a resigned intelligence officer with fifteen years of duplicates was a witness, and he had explained to Cael on a stair what witnesses were. So he had stayed in, and shrunk himself to a posting nobody watched, and filed true empty reports for a decade — true in every particular, empty in every particular that mattered, a decade of a man reporting accurately that there was nothing to report — and carried the case through nineteen shelves, and become the best clerk on a bluff-top academy's counter, and waited.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-14.md:5:The note came up from the assessment office at the seventh bell, on the counter's ordinary stock, in the counter's ordinary hand, addressed to the delegation's floor coordinator and copied nowhere: *Wing three practice allocation revised. Enrollee's supervised hours suspended four days pending recovery-of-form. Reallocation to follow.* It was a true document about a true thing and it said nothing whatever, and Cael read it lying flat on his back on the residence floor with his boots still on, because the floor was the only surface in the room that had stopped moving, and thought, with the part of him that was still able to draft sentences, that the man had built a postponement out of a scheduling instrument in under an hour and had probably not thought of it as difficult.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-14.md:21:It let go on its own. That was the part he wrote down twice. It let go on its own after twelve seconds — spent, the way a spent position releases — and the frame was a frame, and nothing in the room had changed except that a boy who had spent four years insisting his architecture was an instrument had just been given a demonstration of what it was like when the instrument moved first.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-10.md:119:He could count it. That was the part he wrote down and disliked writing. The east gallery's second and third rows had come to their feet for her second exchange; at the third bout's call they applauded the Halcenvane third-year, seated, at half length, in the manner of a room being correct. A steward who had spent the morning finding the delegation chairs found them nothing in the afternoon. Two of the four correspondents' boards left the hall behind the officer.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-16.md:83:"That is exactly the question, and my answer is that I do not know, and that I can prove I cannot know from these documents." Karis's voice did not change at all, which was how they knew she meant it as method and not as caution. "Who built the sub-layer, and when, and why the old word's danger was a danger at all — none of that is in these files, and nothing in an operative's two-century paper trail can reach it, because Seln's files are operational, and the layer beneath the operation is not a thing operations touch. If I put a guess in the register tonight, the guess gets read as a finding by whoever opens the register in ten years, and then we have done to ourselves in miniature exactly what was done to that girl in the river town: written a plausible thing over a true unknown and let the record carry it forward."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-12.md:134:He drafted it at the counter in the ordinary morning, with the counter's lamps lit and two enrollees waiting behind the rail for floor-time stamps, and he stamped both of them in the middle of drafting it, because a document written in one sitting behind a closed door is a document that looks different from the four hundred and nine before it. It went in the office's standard form, in the register fifteen years had polished to instrument-grade: *subject's activity: routine; enrollment defense conducted per schedule; no undisclosed capability observed; term proceeds.* True. Complete in form. Empty.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-18.md:143:Brom asked the only practical question at that table, and there was nothing in it but the arithmetic.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-17.md:15:He said his own last, because he was last, and he said it flat, with nothing in it.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-17.md:109:**He would not report it.** He said the sentence flatly and it landed in the room the way a stone lands in a well, and Cael understood while it was still in the air that he was watching a man cross a line he could not come back over: an Archmarshal, in knowing possession of evidence of the falsification, filing nothing. It was the same crossing a Shadow Path clerk had made at a coastal posting nineteen years earlier and had gone on making every morning for a decade in true empty reports, and now it had been made twice, in two registers, at two altitudes, by the only two men in the machine who had ever had the chance.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-07.md:51:"They will not use it," Karis said. "There's nothing in it to use."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-06.md:115:The proceedings noted it at the second session and set it aside. The clerk read it into the minute; the seal received it; Jent did not object, because there was nothing in it to object to, and the seat did not engage it, because engaging it was not contemplated by the schedule. The rhyme was deliberate and everyone at the respondent's table heard it: a chamber with no windows had received the Compact's best evaluator's testimony with courtesy and set it aside, and now the proceedings had received the continent's best practitioner's and done the same — the two most credentialed voices the system had ever produced, both discounted, in the same grammar, because credentials had never been the currency. Cael filed the rhyme in the log with the flat care he kept for findings about the machine's character: *it doesn't matter who says the instruments are wrong. It doesn't matter how high up the ladder they stand when they say it. The ladder isn't the thing that hears.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-22.md:43:Because a review of an intelligence officer's fifteen years does not ask about his absence. It asks about his access. And his access had been total; and his output, on inspection, was strange. Fifteen years of true, empty, unremarkable reports. Nothing false in any of them. Nothing *in* any of them. And the postings they were written from had all sat, on the map somebody in a quiet room was now drawing for the first time, within reach of anomalies that had later been corrected without fuss.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-22.md:47:What it did instead was requisition. Within the week the officer's whole product was called in from three separate repositories for consolidated review — fifteen years of paper, boxed, carted, and stacked in a room, to be read end to end by people who had never read it once, looking for the thing that would not be in it. They would not find it. Nothing in fifteen years of that product was false, and nothing in it was informative, and a reviewer looking for a lie would go through the whole run and come out the other side with a headache and a conviction he could not evidence — which was precisely the property the reports had been built to have, by a man who had understood, from his first posting, that the safest document in the world is a true one that says nothing.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-22.md:59:It assembled itself complete, in the register the trade had polished — the standard opening, the subject line, the body of three clauses, the closing formula, *subject's activity: routine* — the way a man's hand shapes the letters of a word he has written ten thousand times before the pen has touched the paper. It was accurate. It was, as every one of them had been, entirely true and entirely empty. It had taken him four minutes to compose ten thousand times and it took him four minutes now, walking, with nobody's name on it and nowhere for it to go.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-22.md:95:The man who had written *continue observation* into the file two years ago wrote nothing into it now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:131:Nineteen bundles stood in it on their ends, tied with the office's grey tape, each one his own duplicates from one posting. He had never kept a list of them. He had never needed to. The labels told him: a town's reference, a year, a count of subjects in his small square hand. One academy's label said four. A district seat's said eleven. The newest, on the end of the row, said one, and had the name of this hill on it. Fifteen years of his trade stood in that cupboard on their ends, sixty-three subjects in all, and every page in every bundle was as true and as empty as the return on the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:139:In the registry's file on the boy, he supposed, his returns lay in the same drawer as the hearing's finding and the renewals and the Archmarshal's three sentences. If the boy ever saw them he would count them on his own side. There was nothing in them to object to. Seln had made sure there was nothing in them at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:147:He remembered the room by its east wall, because that was where the strangers had been. Four chairs set out by Bracken at a guest's distance from one another. The delegation's counsel in the nearest, untying a folder's ribbon with her teeth. Ilsev beside her, sharpening a pencil into a fold of paper on her knee. Havel with a board across his knees, writing the date. And in the fourth chair the Archmarshal, with nothing in his hands at all. Three hours of instruments on the oak under those four chairs: the frames, the board, the pendulum ticking in its case. Then Gault reading his note aloud at the trestle, and the Ash instructor signing, and the Mire instructor adding her four lines about the faulty frame, and the clerk bringing the stamp down on the house's copy. *Renewed.* That had been the day, and the house's copy had been stamped on it.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-02.md:19:"*At its discretion.*" She came back to the table and put one finger on the line, and the finger was steady, which he filed. "Part two. *Suspension of practitioner status pending review, such review to be initiated by the registry at its discretion.* I underlined it the first time because I was twenty-nine and I thought it meant the registry would choose when to be merciful." She took the finger away. "I underlined it the second time, six years later, because I had learned what it actually means, which is that nothing in the sequence obliges anybody to ever begin the review. A restriction with no scheduled end is not a punishment. It is a condition." She looked at Bracken. "Registrar. How many words in that chapter set a deadline?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:175:He read her. He did it without deciding to; it was the oldest habit he had. He let the read go out across the court to her, the way he let it go out across a floor before a bout, and it came back with her weight. Settled. Even. Square on both feet, with nothing in it leaning anywhere at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:53:Then he drew it to him, opened it at a place he did not need to look for, laid a strip of plain paper under the first line, and put on his spectacles. He read it flat. Cael had heard him read a hundred pages aloud and never one like this; there was nothing in the voice at all, because Bracken would not let there be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:79:"It's expulsion that follows you," said Withrow. "You can walk out of a town. You can't walk out of the post. It's waiting at the next town, and the one after, and nobody is obliged ever to begin the review that would stop it." She took the brown book from under her arm and held it in both hands. "Twice in my registry years I put my name to one. The first I'd sign again tomorrow; he had done worse than the registry ever learned. The second had a daughter." She looked at the book and not at them. "She came to my counter every week for a year to ask whether the review had begun. Every week I told her the truth, which was that nobody had begun it and nothing in the code obliged anybody to. And every week she thanked me, and went home." A pause. "I have never forgotten her name. Put that down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:115:"Nobody will argue that one," she said. "Not them, not us. A frame at Denvash had nothing to say about him, and every office since has copied the same nothing into its books." She moved on to the sixth, and lifted her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:47:It was Brom's own gift he read him with, taken years ago and used every day since, and it came back to him as it always did, as weight and where the weight went. The bar's load ran down through the crossed forearms and into the shoulders, the right taking a little more than the left, and from there down the long muscles of the back into the hips and through both heels into the floor. It was like watching water find its way down a roof. Nothing in the whole of Brom was pushing back against the bar; he was only giving the weight somewhere to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:79:*Before four it's a promise.* Rooke had said it over the bread, and Cael had written it, and he saw now what she had done with it in the night. A course before its fourth count had nothing in it yet that would bear weight. It was a floor that had been ordered and not delivered. And Lira, at the count of two, came down with her whole weight on the middle of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:39:"An instrument of the registry, under seal," he read from the card, in a voice with nothing in it. "For joint service upon the Chancellor of Halcenvane Academy and upon the enrollee of record whom it names, the two together and in their own persons. Service to be acknowledged in both their hands. The seal to stay whole until it is." He lowered the card. "Are both parties present?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:7:*Fifth-day. Ninth sitting. Forty minutes by the exchange's clock. The house's counsel on the remedy: that no seat has ever before ordered a practitioner into a frame over a provision that stands. Jent: that the code allows it, and the petition asks for nothing the code doesn't give. Both true. The hood wrote for some time and said nothing. We came home.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:33:He had been seventeen for a year. It had been a long one. He had been seventeen at Norhold on the stone with a healer's fingers in his hand, and seventeen on the road home in a cart, and seventeen behind a bell-frame on a gatehouse roof, and seventeen five days ago on his knees in a chalk cut with a cage of points round him. And now he was not. Nothing in him had changed in the night. Nothing ever did, on a birthday. It was only a line in somebody's register moving down a row, but it was his line, and it had moved, and Lira had said it out loud across a refectory table before anybody else could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:241:He opened the door. Seln stood in the corridor in his coat, with his hands at his sides and nothing in them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:219:Then Seln went on across the court to the wing at his even pace. Cael stood where he was in the cold, with nothing in his hands, and found that he had been taught something, and could not have said at what point in the last quarter hour the teaching had happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:61:There was nothing in the house's book about the woman.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:125:Cael was off the bed without any memory of getting off it; his ribs would tell him about it a long while afterward. He was standing in the middle of the room looking at a doorway with nothing in it, and Lira's face on the far side of the nothing, white, with her shoulder still pressed against the air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:133:Lira had stopped pushing against it. She stood in Brom's arm and put her open hand flat on the nothing in the doorway, at the height of her shoulder, and held it there, and looked at Cael through it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:181:It went out of him the way it always did, like a word. The flint smell came up off the chalk. And he felt the point fray, a little, as the horse's line had frayed under Karis's hand: one thread of it, gone. Then the rest of it held. The spark needed something to burn, and the point had almost nothing in it to burn, and he had two contacts in him and no more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:361:It stopped where it was, halfway out of him, and came back, and lay down, the way the clerk at his elbow had once read an answer on a form and blotted it and gone home. The chalk lay on the boards. Karis's two small flames stood either side of it, burning on nothing. Nothing in the air between them had been told to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:5:He lay on his back with the grey of the window on the ceiling and waited for it, the way a man waits for a dog in the next room to bark once it has heard him stir. The ribs told him where they were. The forearm under its dressing itched in a long line from the wrist to the elbow, which the healer said was the skin remembering how to be skin. The latch sat in its keeper and the door stood in its frame, and nothing in him leaned toward either of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:41:He said it after her. Nothing in him had been asking, and it was quiet anyway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:231:He had been kept by that sentence. He knew it as surely as he knew the ribs. Two years of true, empty weeks had gone up out of the wing with his name on the outside fold, and every one of them had said that nothing had happened. That sentence had stood between him and the people who read returns. And here was the same sentence, word for word, clipped to the back of a girl from a river town. In her case it said that the correction had held. Nothing to report. She had gone home smaller and stayed there, and nine weeks of somebody's careful watching had found nothing in her worth writing down, because the thing worth writing down had been taken off her paper before she was old enough to know it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:137:They kept time with the procedure, and a beat behind it, like a second clock in a house that has been set by the first. When the clerk passed up the minute, the hands took the pen and initialled where the clerk's finger had been, and set the pen down. When the schedule was read, they turned one leaf. The shoulders under the grey did not settle or lift. The hood did not turn toward the respondent's table, or the petitioner's, or the benches. In forty minutes he saw nothing move under that hood that the procedure had not asked to move, and nothing in the hands that the procedure had not put there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:165:The seat's seal was pressed into the foot of the minute's first leaf, in the grey wax the registry used for proceedings. He gave it the attention he gave every seal that crossed a table he sat at, which was close attention, because looking at seals was part of the work. It was the seat's own. The registry at Ostrand, the old device with the river and the scale. He had seen it on ten thousand minutes. There was nothing in it, or about it, that he had not seen before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:175:He looked at the ordinary seal on the ordinary minute and felt, very faintly, the shape of a sixth sheet, the way you feel the shape of a word you cannot quite remember. And then he looked for the fact to put on it, and there was no fact. A seat sitting by its seal was lawful. A hood was lawful. A procurator who conceded everything was lawful and, in Havel's experience, sensible. The seal was the seal it should have been. Nothing in this room had done anything that was not in the code.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:51:He blotted it and read it back. It was true and it said nothing, and he understood with a small cold turn of the stomach that he had written it the way Seln wrote a return. He did not cross it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:115:There was nothing in it, and that was what Hesk's letters were for: a buckle and a captain and a dog asleep across a door, and the stove, and something green, and not one word about registries or polite men or what anybody might be building. Hesk wrote from his bench and stayed at the height of his bench, and the letter came up the bluff like a smell of shavings and reminded Cael that there was a whole country in which people's dogs slept on steps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:227:"They have not doubled either." He turned back a page. "In the term before the cut, I could read them weekly by the grade of the man. A junior on the market day, a senior on the sitting day. Since the cut, they are all senior, every bell, and they do nothing." He put his finger under *None*. "Nobody at the gate. Nothing in the post that should not be in it. No new face in town at the inn on the wharf. Not a letter, not a knock, not a rider."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:143:"I can tell you when the policy began, near enough, and that it was a policy, and that it's been kept since by forms. I can tell you that the oldest thing it did was shut a door the old way had left open, and take the name off the people who'd used it. I can't tell you what the old word was for, before. I can't tell you what the reading is, underneath the function, or how old, or how it came to be there to have a race cut beside it. Nothing in that case reaches down that far, not a line." She put the slip on top of the first one. "We've explained the middle of the story. Not the beginning. Entered as a limit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:69:"His settle." He watched her lips. "She needed to watch him stamp and pay for it, start to finish, with nothing in his way. So she gave him nothing in his way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:85:Cael saw it. Lira saw it a hair before he did. The settle was on the man's own fourth count, his own rhythm, the same rhythm he had paid to in the first exchange with nothing in his way. He had to take his own shove back, whether she had given him anything to shove at or not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:93:He went in at the gate and did not look at the place in the court where the woman had stood. He had looked at it enough on the night of the gate. There was nothing in that place but flagstones, and the boy had drawn it all on the oak in chalk, and it had been washed off in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:201:*Four nothings. I have only ever found a man by what he was afraid to lose, and Jent brought nothing into that room that was his to lose. The compliment he paid Karis the way you pay a fee at a counter, the right amount, honestly. Our whole answer he handed back across the table like a parcel addressed to somebody else.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:35:Nothing in it had changed. He had half thought something would, after the recess, after Ostrand and the borrowed office four streets back, as he had half thought the hill would look different on the morning after the case. But the wool exchange smelled of lanolin and damp coats, as it always had. The forty benches ran back to the doors, a quarter full. Lot seventeen was cut into the floor under his left boot. On the platform the hood sat in its place behind the long table, grey and still, and below it the five chairs of the evaluation seat stood in their row with their covers off and five people in them. Four were district evaluators whose names he had heard read once and had written down; and in the middle, with a file open on her knee at its usual angle, sat Ilsev.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:95:It was. A production order was the most ordinary paper a seat could make. Bracken would carry it up the hill tonight in his coat, and Gault would read it in his office behind wing three with his instrument case very straight on the bench beside him, and would say that he had written it down. There was nothing in Gault's notes that could hurt anybody. There was a year of figures in them that breathed and never settled and never went past a red line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:255:He knew a thing she did not. He knew that there was a door, one day wide, that would let him walk out of her house after a ruling and leave nothing in her folder but an entry in an old enrollment book. He knew that he was sitting through every Fifth-day in a room he no longer believed in, in part so that the door would still be there when it was wanted, and her charter clean on the other side of it. He could not tell her any of that. He could not tell her why he had stopped believing in the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:3:He stood in the door, as he had promised, with his hands in his coat and nothing in them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:195:When Cael looked along the bench again, Seln was on it. He was at its middle, with his hands folded and nothing in front of him, and Cael had not heard the step or the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:253:Then he put the notebook away and went down into the heat of the quadrangle, where the whole house was crowding round the boards in the covered walk, and the year was beginning in the ordinary way, as if nothing in it were waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:183:Lira stayed where she was, leaning on the wall, with her eyes half shut, listening to nothing in particular.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:101:The wing's clock went on with its slow tick over the counter. Nothing in the room changed its note. Seln stood with his two hands flat on the copying table and understood, quite calmly, that this was the largest thing that had happened to him since a green corridor twelve years ago, and that it had happened on a weekday morning between the post and the noon bell, and that nobody would ever write it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:183:It was not the last of its kind. He knew that while he was tying the tape. He would write another next week, and the one after, and every week until the year closed, true and empty, in the old pattern, because the pattern was what the office expected and a pattern that changed was a thing somebody wrote down. Nothing in his life was going to look different from the outside. That was the other half of the catechism, the half she had never needed to say aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:27:She got it wrong the second time. She held the third count, but she held it with her shoulders, and a held shoulder told a watcher more than a moving one. The Stone came and stood beside her and did the four counts himself, very slowly, a Silver doing a child's exercise, with nothing in his shoulders at all. On the fourth he did not burst. He only lifted his back heel a finger's breadth off the boards, and it was enough. Cael, at the rail, felt his own weight go the wrong way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:39:"Is good law." She looked at him across the desk. "I don't say that as your chancellor. Before you ever came up our road I had the Greyvane transcript sent from Ostrand, and I went through it with every tool twenty years at the registry gave me, the way I used to go through other people's appeals when I was paid to find what was wrong with them. I came away with nothing. Their paper is the same. Honest work, both of them, and nothing in either to find." She set both hands on the blotter, a hand's width apart. "That's two true things, and they point opposite ways, and I can only stand on one of them. So I've decided which. I'm going to stand on the first, out loud, in front of that seat and the broadsides and every inspector who ever opens that folder, with this whole house on my back, knowing where the second one goes." She did not lower her eyes. "Make a note of the word *choosing*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:53:"I did know it might," she said. "Not the hood. I'd never have guessed the hood. But a folder, yes, one day, and a column with nothing in it." She brought her eyes back to him. "That's what I meant when I told the hall to leave what it is where anybody can check. Somebody checks. Then you find out whether you meant it." She stood. "I meant it. I'd like that to be the last time either of us asks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:81:A mark with no office behind it. A seal with no register under it. They lay side by side on the table in the lamplight, written years apart, in the same hand. He looked at them for a long while, and he did not make anything of them, because there was nothing in the rule that let him. They were two facts. They were on two sheets.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:129:He went up the library stair and told Karis about the index. She went straight to the digests without a word. Twenty minutes later she found that one of her ninety-one, a coastal panel that had sat a member short, carried a year she had taken from the second volume's index, and the index had it a dozen years late. She ruled the year through and wrote the right one above it. It changed nothing in the argument. It went in anyway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:121:He told it the way he had written it, standing against a wall under a lamp in the covered walk with the notebook flat on the stone at the third hour. The archive at Halcenvane on a bad night two years ago, near enough, in the first year he was on the hill. The fire-watch gone the long way round. A man with a bag at the top of the upper flight, in the doorway, with his gloved hand already going into a press he had no business opening. And on the landing above him, in the light, the wing's assistant. He was reading the man a form, one plain line after another, about copying and paper and what a magistrate counted in years, until the man's hand came back out of the press with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:272:Through the window, down in the court, a man in a dark coat came out of the library's shadow at the bindery end and crossed the flags toward the wing. He went at an even pace, neither slow nor quick. He did not take the covered walk. He went straight across the open court in the frost where anybody could have seen him, and nobody did, because nobody looks at a man going home at the tenth bell with nothing in his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:101:"Then we go home on a Seventh-day," said Seln, "and nothing in the world is different, and we keep the case."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:121:"It's a good room," said Lira, from the chair by the stove. She had been there half an hour. "I've been counting, and there's nothing in it to count."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:133:"He came in by the front, not the yard. He walked up the lane from the wharf end, on the gutter side, in grey, with nothing in his hands, and he stopped and read the brass plate by the door, and then he went in past the keeper's window, where the keeper could see him." Seln looked at the door. "By the clock on the landing, eleven minutes early."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:191:Vastin did not move. His left hand lay on his knee, and once it opened and closed. His eyes were on the window, on the cupola, or past it, on nothing in particular, the way a man looks at the sky when he is listening for something a long way off. His face was quite still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:273:The Archmarshal read the station's line, and then he read the margin beside it. There was nothing in the margin; it was clean. The station clerk had written her Path and her first rank in a straight line and left the margin as clean as the oblongs of wood on a borrowed shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:13:He carried a document case in front of his chest in both hands, as a verger carries a book in a procession, so that you saw the case before you saw the man. A clerk walked a pace behind him with nothing in his hands at all. He came at a slow even walk that did not hurry and did not linger, past the river academy's row, whose fighters were looking at their knees, and stopped in front of the house's bench under the square banner, and bowed to the Chancellor at the registry's exact depth.
